WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.060 --> 00:00:05.090
Hi, today I want to talk about Justin
Sung and his advice on language learning.

00:00:05.150 --> 00:00:07.790
Justin Sung is someone
that I have been following.

00:00:08.260 --> 00:00:11.870
He has some very useful advice
about learning in general.

00:00:12.259 --> 00:00:16.229
A lot of it has to do with the
importance of relevance, the

00:00:16.230 --> 00:00:18.069
importance of our curiosity.

00:00:18.360 --> 00:00:23.130
The effectiveness of retrieval,
the benefits of interleaving

00:00:23.140 --> 00:00:24.540
and coming back to things.

00:00:24.580 --> 00:00:27.350
His videos are very well
done, very well illustrated.

00:00:27.380 --> 00:00:31.230
I suggest you go and have a look if
these are subjects of interest to you.

00:00:31.320 --> 00:00:35.569
So, he did a video on language learning
where he was responding to questions

00:00:35.580 --> 00:00:41.000
from two Japanese learners of English,
and he provided some advice to them,

00:00:41.010 --> 00:00:42.590
which I don't consider very useful.

00:00:42.680 --> 00:00:47.265
And so I'm going to talk about this
because I think it illustrates perhaps

00:00:47.275 --> 00:00:50.004
some of the misconceptions that are
out there about language learning.

00:00:51.934 --> 00:00:56.374
So, he begins by referring to three
types of knowledge, declarative

00:00:56.385 --> 00:01:00.354
knowledge, procedural knowledge,
and conditional knowledge.

00:01:00.414 --> 00:01:02.654
Declarative knowledge is sort of a fact.

00:01:02.794 --> 00:01:05.584
Procedural knowledge
is how to do something.

00:01:05.725 --> 00:01:09.535
And the conditional is when
to do something, and he tries

00:01:09.535 --> 00:01:10.995
to apply this to language.

00:01:11.045 --> 00:01:15.035
With most subjects in university
and school and things, there's a lot

00:01:15.035 --> 00:01:17.935
of declarative knowledge, lots of
concepts, lots of things to learn.

00:01:18.005 --> 00:01:20.715
And there is some procedural and
there is some conditional, knowing

00:01:20.715 --> 00:01:22.495
how to do it, solving an equation.

00:01:22.690 --> 00:01:24.980
Knowing when to use the right
formula, things like that.

00:01:25.040 --> 00:01:28.900
With language learning, there is not
that much declarative knowledge, but

00:01:28.900 --> 00:01:31.440
there is a lot of conditional knowledge.

00:01:31.510 --> 00:01:34.360
These distinctions do exist
in a variety of languages.

00:01:34.380 --> 00:01:39.899
Like you may know that in Spanish,
we have saber, yo se hablar, as

00:01:39.899 --> 00:01:45.199
opposed to say, conocer, which is to
know as in palabras, you know, words

00:01:45.259 --> 00:01:49.200
in different languages, they're the
similar concepts of being able to do

00:01:49.200 --> 00:01:52.759
something, knowing something, sometimes
the different words are interchangeable.

00:01:52.965 --> 00:01:56.115
But the fact of the matter is that when
it comes to language, it's all the same.

00:01:56.345 --> 00:01:58.465
It's something that you get used to.

00:01:58.645 --> 00:02:02.514
You cannot get used to using
the language, speaking the

00:02:02.515 --> 00:02:04.725
language without knowing words.

00:02:04.924 --> 00:02:09.934
Once you have some kind of familiarity
with the patterns of the language, then

00:02:09.934 --> 00:02:12.205
you know the procedural part of it.

00:02:12.345 --> 00:02:15.865
It's all one and it's all
absorbed in the same way.

00:02:15.945 --> 00:02:20.160
Largely through input and then, eventually, by speaking the language.

00:02:20.225 --> 00:02:24.155
So I think that bit of
advice was not very useful.

00:02:25.114 --> 00:02:28.674
The second part of the video then
is where he suggests that if you're

00:02:28.674 --> 00:02:32.334
going to learn some words, learn
different forms of the same pattern.

00:02:32.414 --> 00:02:35.085
It's better to think
of all the variations.

00:02:35.144 --> 00:02:37.255
So instead of saying,
hello, my name is Justin.

00:02:37.265 --> 00:02:39.135
Maybe you should also learn, I'm Justin.

00:02:39.165 --> 00:02:40.614
Hi, or hi there.

00:02:40.625 --> 00:02:41.415
Nice to meet you.

00:02:41.510 --> 00:02:44.730
So these are all kind of the same
message, but it's actually, they're

00:02:44.739 --> 00:02:47.280
different words and they're different
structures and they're different syntax.

00:02:47.320 --> 00:02:50.220
And then based on that, someone
could reply to you differently.

00:02:50.280 --> 00:02:53.280
They might say I'm fine, or they
could also say I'm stressed.

00:02:53.319 --> 00:02:54.190
I'm tired.

00:02:54.250 --> 00:02:56.970
So actually we should
learn all of those as well.

00:02:57.144 --> 00:02:59.755
But then the question is,
how do you learn these words?

00:02:59.834 --> 00:03:03.434
At no point does he sort of lay out
the strategy for acquiring these words.

00:03:03.434 --> 00:03:05.165
Do you just say them to yourself?

00:03:05.364 --> 00:03:08.244
And then he very quickly, very
quickly tries to apply this to

00:03:08.244 --> 00:03:12.774
say, learning four different words
around the subject of economics.

00:03:13.005 --> 00:03:15.965
Instead of practicing them in
isolation, we want to be practicing

00:03:15.965 --> 00:03:18.894
them together and in different contexts.

00:03:19.084 --> 00:03:21.715
So let's say you learnt the word
economy, we don't want to put it

00:03:21.715 --> 00:03:25.834
in a sentence and say, I feel that
the economy is suffering right now.

00:03:25.885 --> 00:03:27.554
And the other person responds
with something and you have

00:03:27.554 --> 00:03:28.674
no idea what they've said.

00:03:28.725 --> 00:03:32.034
So we're always thinking about
All the possible pathways that a

00:03:32.034 --> 00:03:35.644
conversation can take so that when
you do go and talk to someone, you're

00:03:35.654 --> 00:03:39.624
able to carry that conversation for
one more step or two more steps.

00:03:39.725 --> 00:03:43.894
He sort of implies that if you learn these
different words, then within a few weeks,

00:03:43.894 --> 00:03:45.494
you'll be able to discuss economics.

00:03:45.584 --> 00:03:48.054
That isn't simply not true.

00:03:48.385 --> 00:03:52.454
And to say, well, just grab a few words
that are, you think are relevant to you.

00:03:52.670 --> 00:03:54.920
Or at some point he suggests
you should go through the day.

00:03:54.920 --> 00:03:57.769
And every time you think of a word
that you may need, write it down.

00:03:57.940 --> 00:03:59.310
And then put a little tally next to it.

00:03:59.340 --> 00:04:01.399
By the end of the week,
you'll have a list of words.

00:04:01.679 --> 00:04:03.709
This word, I needed it 10 times.

00:04:03.760 --> 00:04:05.959
This word, I needed to know it four times.

00:04:06.010 --> 00:04:08.849
You just start with the ones that
were most at the highest tally, right?

00:04:08.850 --> 00:04:09.829
And you just learn those ones.

00:04:09.959 --> 00:04:12.980
That's not very useful because
the reality is that the very

00:04:12.980 --> 00:04:16.730
frequent words show up quite often
and are learned fairly quickly.

00:04:16.730 --> 00:04:21.279
And I'm sure these Japanese girls
know those words, but a lot of the.

00:04:21.719 --> 00:04:25.659
Less frequent words that they need
in order to discuss economics or

00:04:25.680 --> 00:04:28.219
politics or anything that's serious.

00:04:28.250 --> 00:04:31.469
They don't show up that often and
the only way you're gonna learn them

00:04:31.520 --> 00:04:35.419
is if you have a deliberate plan to
do so much listening and reading and

00:04:35.419 --> 00:04:38.729
that you come across these words in
different contexts, not because you wrote

00:04:38.730 --> 00:04:40.149
them down because you thought of it.

00:04:40.239 --> 00:04:41.430
That's how you learn them.

00:04:41.719 --> 00:04:46.970
If you have in your mind Words that
you feel you're going to need, say on

00:04:46.970 --> 00:04:52.160
economics, you can write a whole long
text in your native language, put it on

00:04:52.170 --> 00:04:57.640
Google Translate, get the translation,
uh, imported into LingQ as a lesson.

00:04:58.020 --> 00:05:03.059
LingQ will generate an artificial
intelligence generated audio for that.

00:05:03.339 --> 00:05:06.520
You can then listen to that, save words.

00:05:06.770 --> 00:05:10.229
Review them, listen to it
several times, if in fact you

00:05:10.229 --> 00:05:11.520
are interested in economics.

00:05:11.520 --> 00:05:14.679
But I think that's the final thing
that's missing in Justin Sung's,

00:05:14.760 --> 00:05:18.590
you know, advice to the girls is he
talks a lot in other videos about

00:05:18.590 --> 00:05:21.010
relevance, importance, curiosity.

00:05:21.109 --> 00:05:24.979
Well, he never asks the girls,
what are you interested in?

00:05:24.999 --> 00:05:26.319
What would you like to hear?

00:05:26.784 --> 00:05:32.745
Listen to read in English, because a
key to success in input based learning

00:05:32.775 --> 00:05:38.114
is this idea of not only comprehensible
input, but compelling input, input

00:05:38.114 --> 00:05:42.655
that maybe isn't that comprehensible
can be made more comprehensible.

00:05:42.940 --> 00:05:47.270
The way we do at Ling where you have the
audio, you can break it down into sentence

00:05:47.270 --> 00:05:49.700
by sentence with timestamped audio.

00:05:49.700 --> 00:05:51.599
You can look words up gradually.

00:05:51.599 --> 00:05:55.529
It becomes more comprehensible,
perhaps more important at some

00:05:55.529 --> 00:05:58.940
point, and especially the level that
these, uh, Japanese students are at.

00:05:59.200 --> 00:06:00.370
It should be compelling.

00:06:00.539 --> 00:06:03.719
It should be something that you are
interested in and that can change, of

00:06:03.719 --> 00:06:07.469
course, but if you stay with something
that you are very interested in, which

00:06:07.469 --> 00:06:13.499
is relevant to you, enjoyable to you,
uh, as your interests change or move,

00:06:13.759 --> 00:06:15.330
then you'll move to some other area.

00:06:15.499 --> 00:06:20.289
But in, in each case, you will be
able to work with related vocabulary.

00:06:20.565 --> 00:06:20.605
Okay.

00:06:20.605 --> 00:06:24.285
Coming across similar vocabulary
in different contexts.

00:06:24.565 --> 00:06:27.775
All of the good things that Justin
Sung talks about in some of his other

00:06:28.185 --> 00:06:33.075
videos, relevance and interleaving and
so forth, but it has to be based on

00:06:33.075 --> 00:06:34.785
some sense of what you're interested in.

00:06:34.965 --> 00:06:38.845
And it's not just a matter of
jotting down some words and then,

00:06:38.935 --> 00:06:40.335
as he says, practicing them.

00:06:40.395 --> 00:06:43.465
Well, how do you practice
them or how do you learn them?

00:06:43.655 --> 00:06:46.945
There has to be a methodology
and that methodology is input.

00:06:47.164 --> 00:06:47.884
Compelling.

00:06:48.159 --> 00:06:51.420
Input, and I think that's sort
of the underlying piece that's

00:06:51.420 --> 00:06:53.680
missing in Justin Sung's video.

00:06:53.979 --> 00:06:58.200
Now, very often people
make critical videos.

00:06:58.590 --> 00:07:02.999
People can make critical videos about
me, uh, or leave critical comments.

00:07:03.550 --> 00:07:06.310
And sometimes they say, I
don't mean to be critical.

00:07:06.489 --> 00:07:11.474
Well, I mean to be critical, uh, and
I think it's important to be critical.

00:07:11.635 --> 00:07:17.625
I think we have to judge things
honestly, based on our best knowledge

00:07:17.625 --> 00:07:19.474
of the subject and our prejudices.

00:07:19.774 --> 00:07:21.375
And we say, this is what I think.

00:07:21.534 --> 00:07:24.615
So you have just heard what
I think are Justin Sung's

00:07:24.634 --> 00:07:25.974
video about language learning.

00:07:26.434 --> 00:07:30.455
And I would like to leave you with two
videos that I did about comprehensible

00:07:30.495 --> 00:07:32.285
input and compelling input.

00:07:32.995 --> 00:07:33.775
Thank you for listening.

00:07:33.945 --> 00:07:34.445
Bye for now.

